WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:04.267
When the animation process
for Netflix's "Klaus" began,

00:00:04.267 --> 00:00:06.360
it looked something like this.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:07.630
It eventually started looking

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:10.010
like a pretty impressive 2D film.

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:12.580
But then the animators
went one step further

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.320
to create a film that looked like this.

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:18.070
Suddenly the characters
looked three-dimensional.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:20.550
But unlike most animated
movies these days,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.960
the characters in "Klaus" aren't CGI

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.373
and can't even be considered 3D.

00:00:25.373 --> 00:00:27.732
It's all just a trick of the light.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:34.920
About 300 people, including 40 animators,

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:37.010
worked on the movie "Klaus,"

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.570
which took over two years to make.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.600
And it was completed under the wire,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.440
just one month before
it premiered on Netflix.

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.990
So, why did it take so many
people and so much time?

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:52.120
To understand, we have to go back to 2010,

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.140
long before "Klaus" was
nominated for an Academy Award,

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.650
to when director Sergio
Pablos came up with the idea.

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.690
Because his story was about
the origin of Santa Claus,

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.700
it appealed to nostalgia.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.770
And he thought a nostalgic,
2D animation style

00:01:06.770 --> 00:01:09.000
like we saw in the '90s Disney films

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:10.830
would be a better fit for the story.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.850
But he also wanted to advance the look,

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:15.510
so his team at SPA Studios in Madrid

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:18.620
added a few new crucial steps
to the animation process.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.752
Sergio Pablos: I never looked
at 3D as an evolution of 2D.

00:01:20.752 --> 00:01:22.340
I looked at it as a split,

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.110
like there's a new way
of making animation now.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.550
Narrator: First, they
storyboarded the script

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:31.150
and made a cut using temporary
voices for the characters.

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:32.660
They swapped these out later

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:35.320
once the real cast was recorded.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.540
The next step was layout,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.260
where the team designed backgrounds

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:41.480
and figured out the
placement of the cameras.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.810
Animating the characters
and coloring the backgrounds

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.830
happened simultaneously.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:48.930
The end goal was to have both
blend together seamlessly

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:51.340
and look like they're
part of the same world.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.270
The characters were all hand
drawn using digital tablets

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:57.583
and a program called Harmony by Toon Boom.

00:01:57.583 --> 00:01:59.810
The animators used
live-action reference videos

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.080
of themselves as a guide.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:04.160
The initial sketches were very rough,

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.948
as you can see here.

00:02:05.948 --> 00:02:07.210
But there was a cleanup stage

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:08.960
in which artists refined the drawings

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.420
with crisp, bold lines.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:15.250
Then they painted the characters
with basic flat colors.

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.910
Here, everything still looks very 2D,

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.003
but they will soon bring
the characters to life

00:02:20.003 --> 00:02:21.560
with a very important addition

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.326
usually reserved for 3D animation:

00:02:24.326 --> 00:02:25.370
lighting.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:26.819
His team tested out a new method

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:28.410
of lighting 2D characters

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.610
and released a two-minute, 30-second-long

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:33.690
proof-of-concept teaser back in 2015.

00:02:33.690 --> 00:02:35.840
The proof of concept looked good

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:37.800
and secured them a deal with Netflix,

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.100
but the process was too labor intensive.

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:41.730
So they partnered with a French company

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.220
called Les Films du Poisson Rouge

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:45.967
to help advance the technology,

00:02:45.967 --> 00:02:50.003
which they called KLaS, short
for Klaus Light and Shadow.

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:53.420
Poisson Rouge was able to make
the tool much more efficient

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:56.170
and easier for the artists to work with.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.840
The KLaS tool allows the
artists to paint with light

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:00.600
using a number of
different types of lighting

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.190
in various combinations,

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.530
like "key light" and "ambient light."

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:08.310
With 3D CGI, light is added
automatically to objects,

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:10.200
but it's trickier with 2D.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.960
Pablos: Well, with the
lines, with drawings,

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:13.810
the computer needs a certain level of AI

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:17.227
to even understand that this
line corresponds to this line

00:03:17.227 --> 00:03:19.600
and this hand is also this hand.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.880
Narrator: The software tracks
movement of the characters

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:24.080
so the light and shadows
will move with it.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:27.680
The program takes a very educated guess,

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.830
but it's not 100% accurate,

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:33.100
so the artists can go in
and fine-tune it by hand.

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:34.558
Painting with light allowed the artists

00:03:34.558 --> 00:03:36.683
to get creative with details

00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:39.099
down to the tiniest
reflections in their eyes,

00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:40.330
as you can see here.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.770
The team used lighting not
only to make the characters

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:44.170
feel more real,

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:46.360
but also to help tell the story.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.242
For example, when Jesper is
handing out papers to the kids

00:03:49.242 --> 00:03:50.680
like a drug dealer,

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:52.100
he's always standing in the dark

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.570
to illustrate his shady behavior.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:57.630
And when he's exposed at the
end of the film by his father,

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.560
he's the only one standing in the light,

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.360
while the others are in the dark.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:03.420
Inspiration for detailed
lighting techniques

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.720
came from movies and TV shows,

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.570
like using just a sliver of
light to illuminate a character

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.490
similar to this scene in "Apocalypse Now."

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.260
And this scene, when
Jesper confronts the bully,

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:16.710
was inspired by "Breaking Bad."

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:18.380
It's important that the backgrounds

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.030
also look three-dimensional

00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:22.980
and follow the same lighting
pattern as the characters,

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:25.550
so they used "color keys" as a guide.

00:04:25.550 --> 00:04:27.166
Pablos: They're quick doodles, you know,

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:29.027
they don't have a lot of detail,

00:04:29.027 --> 00:04:31.340
but they tell you exactly
what the light direction is

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.600
and how it's gonna affect both
characters and backgrounds.

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:35.880
Narrator: For example,
this color keys shows

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.770
how Jesper and Alva will be backlit

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:40.030
by the sun coming through the window.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:42.238
And this one shows a
progression of how the light

00:04:42.238 --> 00:04:45.172
will change on Alva as
she steps towards Jesper.

00:04:45.172 --> 00:04:46.710
To make the backgrounds pop

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:49.250
and appear 3D like the characters,

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:51.730
the animators used several
different techniques,

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:54.880
such as multiplanes, where you
have layers on top of layers

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:56.700
to give the illusion of depth.

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:00.920
The team created a total
of 3,160 scenic layouts

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:02.580
for the movie.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:05.160
After they'd merged the
characters with the backgrounds,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.590
they used a second major step

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:08.310
that really gave the 3D characters

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.126
that intricate detail
to bring them to life:

00:05:11.126 --> 00:05:12.301
texture.

00:05:12.301 --> 00:05:13.970
With another tracking tool,

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:16.260
they used contour, lighting, and motion

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.310
to add various effects to
specific parts of a character.

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:20.940
Pablos: So now you could say, well,

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:22.762
I don't want a lot of
roughness on the skin,

00:05:22.762 --> 00:05:25.400
but I want the coat to feel rougher.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.240
Narrator: For example,
they can make them look

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:29.970
like an oil painting or a watercolor.

00:05:29.970 --> 00:05:31.660
These textures are subtle,

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:34.480
but if you look closely, you
can notice the difference.

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:37.430
In the end, the characters
looked much more 3D

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:39.360
and like a part of their environment,

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.030
as opposed to looking like stickers

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:42.970
on top of an elaborate painting.

00:05:42.970 --> 00:05:44.270
Pablos: And that's what throws people off

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:45.420
when they say, "This is 3D,"

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:49.370
because it's volume and it's
moving and it has texture.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:51.630
But it's really a combination of the light

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:54.900
and the texture that makes that illusion.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:57.900
Narrator: The final stage
is final composition,

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.560
in which any last-minute
details are added to the image,

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:01.700
like particles.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:05.160
While the majority of the
film followed the 2D process,

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.350
the animators did use 3D models

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.160
for some characters and objects

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:11.860
and combined the two seamlessly.

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:14.720
And even though these
were created using CGI,

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.610
they were lit the same way
as the 2D characters were:

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:18.620
by hand.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:21.730
Pablos: There's things that
benefit from being drawn

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:23.137
because they feel more organic,

00:06:23.137 --> 00:06:24.970
and there's, you know,

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:26.586
things that are not
supposed to look organic.

00:06:26.586 --> 00:06:27.970
There's things that are
supposed to look solid,

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:30.920
like wagons and doors and props,

00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:33.490
and it's really hard to
make it feel consistent

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:36.170
and solid through drawing.

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:39.745
Narrator: If you look
closely, Jesper's wagon is 3D,

00:06:39.745 --> 00:06:41.530
and so are some of the reindeer.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:42.860
Pablos: Whenever the
reindeers had to do something,

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.560
it was particularly challenging for 3D

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.750
to look right with the 2D.

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:49.790
We just animate the reindeers in 2D,

00:06:49.790 --> 00:06:52.038
and sometimes we would
animate one reindeer in 2D

00:06:52.038 --> 00:06:54.790
and the rest in the shot in 3D.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:56.360
Narrator: Scenes like this chase scene

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:57.560
at the end of the film

00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.600
so seamlessly combine 2D and 3D elements

00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:03.370
that it can be difficult
to tell which is which.

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:04.710
Pablos: There was a shot where Jesper

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:07.693
lifts a plate cover at one point,

00:07:07.693 --> 00:07:10.460
and I commented on how
good the integration

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:14.010
of that 3D plate cover
looked with the 2D actor,

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:16.577
and they said, "Oh, no,
no, that's actually 2D,

00:07:16.577 --> 00:07:18.718
we just painted it to look like metal."

